{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793559257,"nonce":"UHI/1xL+IZzqvUMxc5SiAcqaGKs=","message":"Layer 2, first slice built and committed: merge instead of a Yrs update log. A save refused because the cloud copy moved on offers Merge cloud changes in the browser panel. The merge (native/sync/src/merge.rs, client::merge_cloud, session op sync.merge) is a three-way merge of trees: a file changed on one side moves over; text changed on both sides is merged by Yrs from a base seeded with a fixed client id when the edits are in different places (line granularity; neighbouring lines count as overlapping, as in Git; identical edits count once); the same lines, binaries, files over 1 MiB, edit-versus-delete and added-on-both-sides are reported as conflicts and nothing at all is changed. Chosen by the user: merge different places, report overlaps.\n\nWhy no cloud-side update log: both versions are in hand when two sides meet, so only the common base is needed, and the cloud deletes a generation's chunks once it moves on. The session keeps what its tree is a copy of (capture at start, refreshed after every save or merge, text files up to 1 MiB, 32 MiB total).\n\nAlso fixed: a desktop push used to publish over whatever generation the cloud was at; it now remembers the generation it last synced and refuses as a conflict when the cloud is newer (force still replaces).\n\nNot built: the desktop side of the merge (pull cloud changes into a folder, which needs the folder to keep a base copy too); choosing a side for conflicted files; unsaved editor buffers; continuous Yrs sync of open documents; symlinks are not merged.","files":null}]}